The code statements that lead to the bug are:
x25->fraglen += skb->len;
Reason:
This statement accumulates the length of received fragments without any overflow check. If many packets are received with the `more` flag set, `fraglen` can exceed `USHRT_MAX` (65535), leading to integer overflow. Additionally, the `fraglen` is not reset when the fragment queue is cleared in `x25_clear_queues()`, so stale values persist.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (x25->fraglen + skb->len > USHRT_MAX)
	return 1;
x25->fraglen = 0;
Reason:
The first added line prevents overflow by checking whether adding the new packet length would exceed the maximum allowed value (`USHRT_MAX`) and returns an error if so. The second line resets `fraglen` to zero after successfully processing the final fragment, ensuring the counter is correctly cleared. The commit also mentions resetting `fraglen` in `x25_clear_queues()` to avoid stale values when the queue is purged.

